# ==========================================================================
# MEDIA CODEC PROFILE — the ONE home of the encoding contract MC directs and
# MH costs against (story 3 R-23, R-24; ADR-0036 §11 "Derive rather than
# guard"). Production defaults. Every value below exists ONLY here: MC and MH
# read these keys by `configMapKeyRef` (key == env name), and no constant in
# code, no other ConfigMap and no manifest comment restates them.
#
# MEMBERSHIP RULE — read before adding a key. A value belongs in this file
# ONLY if both MC and MH must agree on it. The test to apply: "would MH's
# derived costs, or its validation, be wrong if this value changed without MH
# rolling?" If not, it is not profile material: an MC-only knob goes in
# infra/services/mc-service/config.env, an MH-only knob in
# infra/services/mh-service/config.env. (A header rule, not a guard.)
# The codec and geometry keys are read by MC alone and still belong: the
# profile is ONE encoding contract, the bitrate and frame-rate caps are caps OF
# that codec at that geometry, and MH's derived cost is only valid for the
# encoding the profile describes.
#
# CHANGING ANY VALUE HERE IS A COORDINATED MC+MH ROLL. This ConfigMap is
# content-addressed (ADR-0038 §2) and referenced from mc-0, mc-1, mh-0 and
# mh-1, so any edit changes all four pod templates. That includes the keys
# only MC reads (codec, width, height): editing one still rolls MH. An MH roll
# SHEDS EVERY MEDIA SESSION ON THE POD (ADR-0036 §11) — accepted here because an
# encoding change is a coordinated cutover anyway. It is exactly why the R-26
# video-off lever, `MC_MAX_VIDEO_RECEIVE_SLOTS`, is deliberately NOT in this
# file: pulling a lever must never shed MH sessions.
#
# THE COST MODEL THIS FEEDS, AND THAT IT IS INTERIM. MH derives each per-stream
# egress cost from this file, for audio and video alike:
#   cost = MAX_BITRATE_BPS + per-frame overhead x FRAME_RATE_HZ
# with the overhead derived in MH code from media-protocol size constants
# (frame header, relay region, signature, SFrame overhead; plus the wrapped
# transmit key on audio frames) — `EgressAdmission` in
# crates/mh-service/src/config.rs. There is no headroom knob. MH stays
# type-blind: its stream ceiling divides the budget by the LARGER derived
# cost, so raising MEDIA_VIDEO_MAX_BITRATE_BPS or MEDIA_VIDEO_FRAME_RATE_HZ
# LOWERS every MH's stream ceiling — a profile edit is a capacity change.
# No derived figure is written here on purpose: read it from
# `mh_media_egress_stream_ceiling` (and MH's startup log once MH-A1 lands).
# This bitrate-derived model is INTERIM (user capacity note, 2026-10-08): see
# docs/decisions/adr-0036-media-flow.md §"Addendum — Planned Story Arc", item
# 9 "Adaptive capacity". Do not tune it as if it were the long-term design.
#
# READERS. MC reads all nine for the send directive once MC-A lands. MH reads
# the five cost/keyframe keys: the four bitrate/frame-rate keys once MH-A1
# lands, MEDIA_VIDEO_MIN_KEYFRAME_INTERVAL_MS once MH-B lands. The CURRENT
# images ignore every key in this file. Every key is a required read with no
# code default in its readers, and every reader's pod holds a hard
# configMapKeyRef, so an absent key is a loud CreateContainerConfigError.
# Bounds are owned by the readers and are cited, never restated.
# ==========================================================================

# Video codec MC directs publishers to encode with (ADR-0036 §5).
# Enforced by: mc-service at config load once MC-A lands — parsed into the
# closed `Codec` proto enum; an unknown value or an audio codec fails startup,
# never falls back. The current images ignore this key.
# Where an operator sees it: MC's startup log of the effective directive
# parameters; a browser that cannot encode or decode it fails loudly at media
# start (R-8).
MEDIA_VIDEO_CODEC=vp8
# Video frame width, in pixels, MC directs.
# Enforced by: mc-service at config load once MC-A lands (bounds in
# crates/mc-service/src/media_signaling/directive.rs, added by MC-A). The
# current images ignore this key. MH does not read geometry.
# Where an operator sees it: MC's startup log of the directive parameters.
MEDIA_VIDEO_WIDTH=640
# Video frame height, in pixels, MC directs. As MEDIA_VIDEO_WIDTH.
MEDIA_VIDEO_HEIGHT=360
# Video frames per second MC directs, and one of MH's two video cost inputs.
# Enforced by: mc-service at config load once MC-A lands (bounds in
# directive.rs, added by MC-A); mh-service reads it to derive the per-stream
# video cost once MH-A1 lands. The current images ignore this key.
# Raising it raises MH's derived video cost and LOWERS every MH's stream
# ceiling (see the header).
# Where an operator sees it: `mh_media_egress_stream_ceiling`.
MEDIA_VIDEO_FRAME_RATE_HZ=30
# Maximum video bitrate MC directs, in bits per second; the dominant term of
# MH's derived video cost.
# Enforced by: mc-service at config load once MC-A lands (bounds in
# directive.rs, added by MC-A); mh-service reads it to derive the per-stream
# video cost once MH-A1 lands. The current images ignore this key.
# NOT the same quantity as MH's derived per-stream cost (bitrate plus framing,
# published only through `mh_media_egress_stream_ceiling`) and NOT
# `MH_EGRESS_BUDGET_BPS` (a whole-pod budget). DO NOT UNIFY THEM.
# Where an operator sees it: `mh_media_egress_stream_ceiling`.
MEDIA_VIDEO_MAX_BITRATE_BPS=800000
# Minimum interval, in milliseconds, between keyframes a publisher produces on
# request (R-11; story 3 R-23 amendment). There is no keyframe INTERVAL — this
# is a floor on how often a request can be honoured.
# MC carries it on the video directive as `min_keyframe_interval_ms`. The
# value is SERVER-CONFIGURED, never client-reported (a client-reported value
# would let a patched client suppress the keyframe-storm alert); the client
# honours requests at most once per max(directed, its compiled floor).
# Enforced by: mc-service at config load once MC-A lands
# (`MIN_KEYFRAME_INTERVAL_MS_FLOOR`/`_CEILING` in directive.rs, added by
# MC-A); and mh-service once MH-B lands, which bounds it too and REFUSES TO
# BOOT unless `MH_KEYFRAME_REQUEST_RETRY_MS` (mh-service/config.env) is at
# least this value — a retry faster than the publisher's minimum interval is
# wasted. Equality is legal. Required and nonzero in both readers. The current
# images ignore this key.
# CROSS-FILE COUPLING: once MH-B lands, raising this above
# MH_KEYFRAME_REQUEST_RETRY_MS makes MH refuse to boot, so both must change in
# the same deploy — and because this is a profile change, it rolls MC and MH
# together. `MH_KEYFRAME_REQUEST_AGGREGATION_MS` is deliberately NOT checked
# against it (aggregation coalesces receivers' requests; it does not pace the
# publisher).
# Where an operator sees it: `mc_media_video_keyframe_min_interval_seconds`
# (added by MC-A; the value MC directs).
MEDIA_VIDEO_MIN_KEYFRAME_INTERVAL_MS=500
# Audio codec MC directs (ADR-0036 §5). Parsed into the closed `Codec` proto
# enum: an unrecognised value, "unspecified" or a video codec fails startup,
# never falls back to Opus.
# Enforced by: mc-service at config load once MC-A lands. The current images
# ignore this key (the current MC image reads the retired `MC_AUDIO_CODEC`).
# Where an operator sees it: MC's startup log of the directive parameters.
MEDIA_AUDIO_CODEC=opus
# Maximum audio bitrate MC directs, in bits per second; also an input to MH's
# derived audio cost.
#
# MH NOW READS THIS VALUE rather than assuming it. mh-service sizes its
# operator-facing datagram-buffer budget (a LATENCY ceiling) at the floor of
# its compiled supported audio bitrate band, so once MH-A1 lands it REFUSES TO
# BOOT if this value lies outside `SUPPORTED_AUDIO_BITRATE_FLOOR_BPS..=
# SUPPORTED_AUDIO_BITRATE_CEILING_BPS` (crates/mh-service/src/config.rs) —
# what was a silent mis-size is now a boot refusal. MC bounds it with
# `AUDIO_BITRATE_MIN_BPS`/`AUDIO_BITRATE_MAX_BPS`
# (crates/mc-service/src/media_signaling/directive.rs). The deployable value
# is the intersection of the two.
#
# RELATED BUT DIFFERENT — DO NOT UNIFY THEM:
#   - this key: the codec maximum directed to the publisher;
#   - MH's supported-band constants: a compile-time buffer-sizing envelope;
#   - MH's derived per-stream audio cost: published only through
#     `mh_media_egress_stream_ceiling`.
# (`MH_STREAM_COST_AUDIO_BPS` is retired, superseded by that derived cost.)
# Enforced by: mc-service at config load once MC-A lands; mh-service at config
# load once MH-A1 lands. The current images ignore this key (the current MC
# image reads the retired `MC_AUDIO_MAX_BITRATE_BPS`).
# Where an operator sees it: `mh_media_egress_stream_ceiling` (through the
# derived cost) and MC's startup log.
MEDIA_AUDIO_MAX_BITRATE_BPS=48000
# Audio frames per second MC directs; also an input to MH's derived audio
# cost. This rate IS the frame duration expressed in the reciprocal unit.
#
# MC'S BOUND is `AUDIO_FRAME_RATE_MIN_HZ..=AUDIO_FRAME_RATE_MAX_HZ` in
# directive.rs. Its low end (40 ms frames) is ADR-0036 §3's named
# signature-overhead mitigation, so it is a live path rather than a
# theoretical bound — and moving there is a coordinated change with
# mh-service, never a unilateral one.
#
# MH'S RULE IS AN EQUALITY, NOT A RANGE. mh-service reports its datagram-buffer
# budget to operators as a LATENCY derived from its compiled
# `AUDIO_FRAME_DURATION_MS`, so once MH-A1 lands it REFUSES TO BOOT unless this
# value is exactly the rate that duration implies. The deployable value is
# therefore MH's single value; a lower rate needs an MH code change first.
# WHY: the buffer is a BYTE budget, and a lower frame rate grows each frame by
# less than its duration grows (the Opus payload scales, the fixed header and
# crypto overhead do not — see MH's derivation of `NOMINAL_AUDIO_FRAME_BYTES`),
# so fewer, longer frames would inflate the real held latency past the
# reported figure, silently, in the one service whose ADR-0036 §1 goal is to
# prefer loss to unbounded latency. MH reading this key turns that silent skew
# into a loud refusal.
# Enforced by: mc-service at config load once MC-A lands; mh-service at config
# load once MH-A1 lands. The current images ignore this key (the current MC
# image reads the retired `MC_AUDIO_FRAME_RATE_HZ`).
# Where an operator sees it: MC's startup log; a mismatch is MH refusing to
# start (`kubectl logs --previous` names the variable).
MEDIA_AUDIO_FRAME_RATE_HZ=50
